<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Caucasus</p><h1 class="st-title">Kuban Cossack Host</h1><p class="st-meta">1860 CE – 1920 CE · capital Yekaterinodar</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Cossack community of the Kuban, descended largely from the Zaporozhian Cossacks, which garrisoned the north-western Caucasus for Russia.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Kuban <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cossacks">Cossack</a> Host was formed in 1860 by merging the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/black-sea-cossack-host">Black Sea Cossack Host</a>, made up of former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaporozhian-cossacks">Zaporozhian Cossacks</a> resettled on the Kuban in 1792, with part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus-line-cossack-host">Caucasus Line Cossack Host</a>. It was centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krasnodar">Yekaterinodar</a>, modern Krasnodar.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cossacks">Cossacks</a> held land in stanitsas in return for military service, electing village atamans while the host's chief ataman was appointed by the tsar. They provided cavalry and the plastun scout units and settled lands cleared of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/circassians">Circassians</a> after 1864. Many spoke a Ukrainian dialect, and the Kuban grew rich on grain. Their choir and traditions survive.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After 1917 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuban-rada">Kuban Rada</a> declared autonomy and allied with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/white-movement">Whites</a>. The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/russian-sfsr">Russian SFSR</a> conquered the region in 1920, abolished the host and persecuted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cossacks">Cossacks</a>. The host was revived as a cultural and service organisation in the 1990s.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kuban-cossack-host.jpg" alt="Kuban Cossack Host" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Froztbyte · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Kuban_People%27s_Republic.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1860 CE – 1920 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Yekaterinodar</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Alexander Filimonov</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Russian Orthodox Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Russian; Ukrainian (Kuban dialect)</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kuban Cossacks, Kuban Cossack Army</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1890&amp;focus=kuban-cossack-host" aria-label="Kuban Cossack Host on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5646 217 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kuban Cossack Host lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6487 457h0M5927 843h0M5820 533h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5966 473h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M5966 473h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Russia. <a href="/atlas?year=1890&amp;focus=kuban-cossack-host">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
